course: dictionary
lesson: 12554
title: Devices: machines and tricks
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A device is a machine or tool made for a special job. Devices is the plural, so it means more than one. In today's world, the most common devices are phones, tablets and laptops. «Please switch off your devices before bed.» Here devices means electronic machines like phones and tablets.
explain: Devices can also mean a clever trick or method. In reading and writing lessons you will meet literary devices, which are tricks writers use to make their writing more interesting. «The moon was a silver coin in the sky» compares the moon to a coin. That comparison is a literary device.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. If the sentence talks about screens, charging, rules or the internet, devices means machines. If it talks about a writer, a poem or a story, it probably means tricks. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: A device is a machine or tool made for a special job.
fact: Devices is the plural of device, so it means more than one device.
fact: In online safety lessons, devices means phones, tablets and computers.
fact: A literary device is a trick writers use, such as a simile.
fact: «Leave someone to their own devices» means let them do what they want without help.
exercise: «Please switch off your devices before bed.» What are devices here? | *Phones, tablets and laptops | Bedtime stories | Bedrooms and beds
exercise: «The poem uses a simile, a literary device that compares two things.» What does device mean here? | A bird that sings at night | *A clever trick a writer uses | A machine that makes a loud noise
exercise: «Ella said, "Leave Sam to his own devices," so he built the den alone.» What does the phrase mean? | *Let him do it without help | Give him a tool to use | Tell him to stop
test: What is a device? | *A machine or tool made for a special job | A bird that sings at night | A kind of weather
test: «Our class has rules for screen time, and every device must be charged in the hall.» What does devices mean here? | Stories in books | *Electronic machines such as phones and tablets | Bombs
exam: «The writer used many devices, such as similes, to make the story vivid.» What are the devices? | Machines in the classroom | Gadgets for cooking | *Tricks that writers use to make their writing more interesting
